    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "America's First Successful\nHelicopter Flight\nSeptember 14, 1939 • Stratford, Connecticut, U.S.A.\nRWANDA\n60F\nSIKORSKY VS 300 (1939)\n14\n1984\n06497\nUSA\n20c",
      "International Tributes to the History of Flight\nAmerica's First Successful Helicopter Flight\nA native of Russia, Igor Sikorsky came to America in field. Finally, it flew.\n1919 and achieved great success as a designer of\nfixed-wing aircraft. On the side, he continued to ex-\nThe full-color cachet pictures Sikorsky piloting the\nperiment with designing helicopters — a hobby he\nprototype of the VS-300 on its maiden flight. Over the\nhad begun in 1909 in Russia. By combining the ideas\nnext four years, the helicopter was worked over and\nof other pioneers with his own innovations, he de-\nmodified so completely that only a handful of its\nveloped the experimental VS-300 helicopter.\noriginal parts were left intact.\nOn the morning of September 14, 1939, Sikorsky\nMint Stamp: The mint stamp on this Cover was is-\nand several associates assembled on a field in Strat-\nsued in Rwanda in 1978. It features a portrait of Igor\nford, Connecticut. As Sikorsky climbed aboard the\nSikorsky (1889-1972) along with another version of\nVS-300, the others got down on their knees to see\nthe pace-setting VS-300 helicopter.\nwhether all four wheels of the contraption were off\nPostmark: This Cover was postmarked in Stratford,\nlooking machine banged and bounced around the\nConnecticut on September 14, 1984, the anniversary\nof the flight of America's first successful helicopter.\n© Postal Commemorative Society\nPrinted in U.S.A.\nW1G.056.037"
    ],
